WEBVTT

00:00.540 --> 00:05.040
This is Foldback: decision trees for people
decisions. Should we replace someone?

00:05.040 --> 00:08.262
How do we respond when a key person gets an
outside offer?

00:08.513 --> 00:12.847
It's based on Andrew Marritt's article,
'Should you replace Sam?', the example on
screen.

00:12.847 --> 00:17.473
You lay out your options, what could happen
after each one, and what each path costs or
brings.

00:18.416 --> 00:19.955
Foldback folds the tree back.

00:19.955 --> 00:26.587
'Where it stands' says which option leads, by
how much, and how often it comes out best
across ten thousand plausible worlds.

00:26.837 --> 00:31.024
'What would change your mind' gives the single
number at which the leader would change.

00:31.024 --> 00:34.008
And everything runs in your browser. Nothing
is sent anywhere.

00:34.510 --> 00:38.204
Starting from a blank page is hard, so let's
use 'Build it with me'.

00:38.204 --> 00:39.943
It asks one question per screen.

00:40.143 --> 00:41.066
Here's the case.

00:41.066 --> 00:47.121
Petra, a senior data engineer on seventy
thousand dollars, has an outside offer for
twenty per cent more.

00:47.121 --> 00:48.159
How do we respond?

00:49.798 --> 00:54.392
Three options: match the pay, promote her to
tech lead, or let her go and hire someone new.

00:56.527 --> 01:00.833
Next, the yardstick. I'll keep 'things as they
are today', over the next two years.

01:00.833 --> 01:03.271
The yardstick doesn't change which option
wins.

01:03.271 --> 01:05.554
It only has to be the same for every option.

01:07.958 --> 01:11.559
Now each option in turn. A counter-offer could
go more than one way:

01:11.559 --> 01:16.485
she accepts and stays, she accepts but leaves
within a year, or she turns it down and
leaves.

01:18.276 --> 01:20.806
Nothing is paid for sure, whichever way it
goes.

01:22.379 --> 01:25.222
Chances are asked as how many cases out of a
hundred.

01:25.222 --> 01:29.943
You give the fewest and the most you could
believe first, and only then the most likely.

01:29.943 --> 01:33.162
Thinking about the ends first curbs anchoring
on one number.

01:33.364 --> 01:34.957
There's no row for the last way.

01:34.957 --> 01:38.442
It takes the rest, and this line shows what's
left for it as you type.

01:40.834 --> 01:46.120
Then, what each way costs or brings. You pick
cost or gain, rather than typing a minus sign.

01:46.120 --> 01:50.314
And each answer gets an evidence tag: data,
expert judgement, or a guess.

01:52.263 --> 01:56.251
If she accepts but leaves within a year, we've
paid more and still have to replace her.

01:57.769 --> 01:59.848
If she turns it down, we replace her now.

02:02.729 --> 02:07.586
The promotion: either she takes it and stays,
or she leaves anyway. Again, nothing for sure.

02:09.455 --> 02:14.846
We don't know whether she wants to lead, so
the chance she takes it is wide, twenty to
sixty in a hundred.

02:14.846 --> 02:16.168
And it's honestly a guess.

02:17.694 --> 02:22.205
The raise that comes with the role is a small
cost. If she leaves anyway, we replace her.

02:27.642 --> 02:31.549
Letting her go is settled: one path, the cost
of hiring a replacement.

02:34.758 --> 02:39.047
The last screen reads everything back in plain
words, so you can check it before building.

02:42.128 --> 02:44.315
And here's the tree, built from those answers.

02:45.627 --> 02:50.711
Promoting her to tech lead leads, by three
thousand two hundred dollars over the
counter-offer.

02:50.711 --> 02:55.367
But it's best in only sixty-six per cent of
plausible worlds, so it's far from settled.

02:55.367 --> 02:56.758
Letting her go comes last.

02:57.260 --> 02:58.743
What would change your mind?

02:58.743 --> 03:05.363
If the chance she takes the promotion and
stays falls below thirty-three per cent,
against forty now, the counter-offer wins.

03:05.363 --> 03:07.269
And that's inside the range we gave.

03:07.870 --> 03:13.369
The chart shows each option's likely value,
how widely it could land, and how often it
comes out best.

03:13.870 --> 03:16.252
Under 'Go deeper': what is worth finding out.

03:16.252 --> 03:20.751
Pinning down the cost if she leaves anyway is
worth about a thousand dollars at most.

03:20.751 --> 03:22.339
Everything else is worth less.

03:23.039 --> 03:26.787
So the decision hinges on one thing: whether
Petra really wants to lead.

03:26.787 --> 03:28.922
That's cheap to find out. Go and ask her.

03:29.424 --> 03:32.082
Foldback is free to use, at the address on
screen.
